%I A054862 #16 Oct 18 2026 09:53:27
%S A054862 105,45045,237405,101846745,121486365,274680671265
%N A054862 (+2)-unitary sigma 3-multiple perfect number: numbers k such that (+2)usigma(k) = 3*k, where (+2)usigma(k) = A107759(k).
%C A054862 From _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 17 2026: (Start)
%C A054862 The numbers k that divide A107759(k) include k = 1 (with quotient A107759(k)/k = 1), 2 (with quotient 2), the terms in this sequence (with quotient 3), and twice these terms (with quotient 6). Are other quotients possible?
%C A054862 The numbers k that divide A107759(k) are analogous to unitary multiply-perfect numbers (A327158), with the sum of unitary divisors of the unitary divisors (A107759) instead of the sum of unitary divisors (A034448). (End)
%e A054862 105 = 3*5*7 is a term since (+2)usigma(105) = (2+3)*(2+5)*(2+7) = 315 = 3*105.
%e A054862 Factorizations: 3*5*7, 3^2*5*7*11*13, 3*5*7^2*17*19, 3^2*5*7^2*11*13*17*19, 3^3*5*7*11*13*29*31, 3^3*5*7^2*11*13*17*19*29*31.
%t A054862 s[n_] := Times @@ (2 + Power @@@ FactorInteger[n]); s[1] = 1; Select[Range[2.5*10^5], s[#] == 3*# &] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 26 2022 *)
%o A054862 (PARI) isok(k) = vecprod([f[1]^f[2] + 2 | f <- factor(k)~]) == 3*k; \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 17 2026
%Y A054862 Cf. A034448, A052396, A107759, A327158.
%K A054862 nonn,more,changed
%O A054862 1,1
%A A054862 _Yasutoshi Kohmoto_
%E A054862 Offset corrected by _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 26 2022
